course: dictionary
lesson: 7507
title: Giving to gain respect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A potlatch is a big ceremony and feast held by First Nations and Native American peoples of the Pacific Northwest coast of North America, such as the Kwakwaka'wakw and the Haida. It is a noun, so you can have one potlatch or many potlatches.
explain: At a potlatch, the host gives gifts to guests to mark important events such as births, marriages, names and deaths. «Among peoples of the Pacific Northwest, chiefs held potlatch feasts where they gave away food, blankets and goods.»
explain: Here, giving away is not losing. «On the Pacific Northwest Coast of Canada, chiefs won respect by giving huge gifts at feasts called potlatches.» The clues are gifts, feasts and respect, and they show that wealth was shared to earn status.
fact: A potlatch is a traditional ceremony and feast of First Nations and Native American peoples on the Pacific Northwest coast.
fact: At a potlatch, status and respect came from giving away wealth, not from keeping it.
fact: Canada banned the potlatch from 1885 to 1951, which harmed these cultures.
fact: Phrases you may meet in history lessons include «hold a potlatch», «potlatch ceremony» and «potlatch ban».
fact: Today, potlatches are held again with pride.
exercise: What is a potlatch? | A school sports day in Canada | A kind of fishing boat used on the coast | A sort of sweet bread | *A big traditional ceremony and feast of Pacific Northwest peoples
exercise: At a potlatch, what does the host do? | *Gives gifts to guests to mark important events | Keeps all the gifts for the family | Hides from the guests | Sells food at a market
exercise: «Canada banned the potlatch from 1885 to 1951.» In which year did the ban start? | 1951 | *1885 | 1960 | 1900
test: Why did chiefs give away huge gifts at potlatches? | To make their guests sad | Because they had no use for it | *To win respect and status | To lose their wealth
test: What is a potlatch ban? | A kind of gift | *A rule that stopped potlatches from being held | A party with music | A ceremony for new boats
exam: How did the potlatch ban affect these cultures, and what happens today? | The ban had no effect, and potlatches were never held again | *It stopped important ceremonies for many decades, and today potlatches are held again with pride | It made potlatches bigger and more common | It only stopped gifts from being given to children
